window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["04-1131"]={"d":"04-1131","id":"2005/04-1131","n":"Whitman v. Dept. of Transportation","src":["osg"],"briefs":[{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief for the respondents","date":"","url":"https://www.justice.gov/osg/media/227746/dl?inline","side":"res","src":"osg","fmt":"PDF","detail":"https://www.justice.gov/osg/brief/whitman-v-dot-brief-merits","n":214}],"opinion":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep547/usrep547512/usrep547512.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Abbott v. United States","cite":"144 F.3d 1","y":1998,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/144/1/","by":{"0":"24, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Abbott Labs. v. Gardner","cite":"387 U.S. 136","y":1967,"id":"1966/39","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court found that congress did not intend to forbid pre-enforcement review of regulations like the \"every time\" rule. The legislature designed the statute's specific review provisions to provide additional remedies for parties in Abbott Laboratories' position, not to cut down traditional channels of review. The Court remanded the case to the court of appeals.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"Aircraft & Diesel Equip. Corp. v. Hirsch","cite":"331 U.S. 752","y":1947,"id":"scdb/1946-124","sc":1,"sum":"1. Pursuant to the First and Second Renegotiation Acts, the Secretary of War and the War Contracts Adjustment Board, respectively, determined that appellant had realized excessive profits during two years on subcontracts under which it had supplied parts to contractors manufacturing war equipment for the Army; and the Under Secretary of War directed certain of appellant's customers to withhold and pay into the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Alabama Pub. Serv. Comm’n v. Southern Ry.","cite":"341 U.S. 341","y":1951,"id":"scdb/1950-079","sc":1,"sum":"By special leave of Court, Merton Roland Nachman, Jr., Assistant Attorney General of Alabama, pro hac vice, and Richard T. Rives argued the cause for appellants. With them on the brief was Si Garrett, Attorney General. A. A. Carmichael, then Attorney General, and Wallace L. Johnson, then Assistant Attorney General, were also on a brief with Mr. Rives.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Alexander v. Gardner-Denver Co.","cite":"415 U.S. 36","y":1974,"id":"1973/72-5847","sc":1,"sum":"An employee's statutory right to trial de novo under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 is not foreclosed by prior submission of his claim to final arbitration under the nondiscrimination clause of a collective-bargaining.agreement.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23, 24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Asociacion De Empleados v. Panama Canal Comm’n","cite":"329 F.3d 1235","y":2003,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/329/1235/","by":{"0":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Association of Data Processing Serv. Orgs., Inc. v. Camp","cite":"397 U.S. 150","y":1970,"id":"1969/85","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioners, which provide data processing services to businesses generally, challenge a ruling by the Comptroller of the Currency permitting national banks, such as respondent bank, as an incident to their banking services, to make data processing services available to other banks and bank customers. The District Court dismissed the complaint, holding that petitioners lacked standing to bring the suit, and the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Barrentine v. Arkansas-Best Freight Sys., Inc.","cite":"450 U.S. 728","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-2006","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioners' wage claims under the FLSA are not barred by the prior submission of their grievances to the contractual dispute-resolution procedures.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"22, 24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bennett v. Spear","cite":"520 U.S. 154","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-813","sc":1,"sum":"In the unanimous decision, announced by Justice Antonin Scalia, the Court ruled that the petitioners had standing to ask for judicial review of the minimum water level setting under the Endangered Species Act. The Act explicitly allows \"any person\" to sue the government over an alleged violation. Justice Scalia asserted this applies to the Secretary's actions over enforcement.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Berrios v. Department of the Army","cite":"884 F.2d 28","y":1989,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/884/28/","by":{"0":"26, 32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Billop v. Department of the Air Force","cite":"725 F.2d 1160","y":1984,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/725/1160/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bivens v. Six Unknown Named Agents of Fed. Bureau of Narcotics","cite":"403 U.S. 388","y":1971,"id":"1970/301","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that Bivens does have a cause of action for damages arising from the federal agents Fourth Amendment violations. Bivens must provide proof of his injuries in order to recover. The Court did not reach the privilege question because the Court of Appeals did not consider the question.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Block v. Community Nutrition Inst.","cite":"467 U.S. 340","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-458","sc":1,"sum":"The individual consumers may not obtain judicial review of the milk market orders in question.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"15, 18, 19, 38"}},{"t":"case","label":"Booth v. Churner","cite":"532 U.S. 731","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1964","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that even though the prison grievance procedure did not provide for requested monetary relief, Booth was nonetheless required to exhaust administrative remedies before filing suit with respect to prison conditions.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bowen v. Michigan Acad. of Family Physicians","cite":"476 U.S. 667","y":1986,"id":"1985/85-225","sc":1,"sum":"In neither 42 U. S. C. § 1395ff (1982 ed. and Supp. II) nor § 1395ii (1982 ed., Supp. II), has Congress barred judicial review of regulations promulgated under Part B of the Medicare program.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"42, 47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Broadway v. Block","cite":"694 F.2d 979","y":1982,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/694/979/","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Brown v. GSA","cite":"425 U.S. 820","y":1976,"id":"1975/74-768","sc":1,"sum":"Section 717 provides the exBROWN v. GSA","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco & Firearms v. FLRA","cite":"464 U.S. 89","y":1984,"id":"1983/82-799","sc":1,"sum":"The FLRA's interpretation of § 7131(a) constitutes an \"unauthorized assumption by [the] agency of [a] major policy decisio[n] properly made by Congress,\" American Ship Building Co. v. NLRB, 380 U. S.300, 318.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bush v. Lucas","cite":"462 U.S. 367","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-469","sc":1,"ar":4,"sum":"Not under the CSC regulations. The federal judiciary, pursuant to its common law authority, has the power to recognize new causes of action in absence of, or to supplement, statutory remedies unless Congress has expressly indicated its relief is to be exclusive.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Califano v. Sanders","cite":"430 U.S. 109","y":1977,"id":"1976/75-1443","sc":1,"sum":"1. Section 10 of the Administrative Procedure Act (APA), providing generally for judicial review of actions of federal administrative agencies by persons aggrieved by such actions, does not afford an implied grant to district courts of subject-matter jurisdiction to review a decision of the Secretary of Health, Education, and Welfare not to reopen a previously adjudicated claim for social security benefits. An …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Camp v. Pitts","cite":"411 U.S. 138","y":1973,"id":"1972/72-864","sc":1,"sum":"The appropriate standard for judicial review of a decision by the Comptroller of the Currency denying a national bank charter is whether his adjudication was \"arbitrary, capricious, an abuse of discretion or otherwise not in accordance with law.\" The District Court is to review the administrative record already in existence, supplemented if necessary by affidavits or testimony amplifying the reason for the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"50"}},{"t":"case","label":"Carducci v. Regan","cite":"714 F.2d 171","y":1983,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/714/171/","by":{"0":"3, 20, 22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Carter v. Gibbs","cite":"909 F.2d 1452","y":1990,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/909/1452/","by":{"0":"17, 24, 25, 31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Christian v. New York State Dep’t of Labor","cite":"414 U.S. 614","y":1974,"id":"1973/72-5704","sc":1,"sum":"Any decision upon appellants' statutory or constitutional claims would be premature in view of the fact that the record does not disclose that the state agency, in notifying appellants of the adverse determinations, informed them,'as it was required to do by the applicable regulation, of their \"right to additional information or reconsideration and correction\" of thefindings by the employing agencies; or that …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of San Diego v. Roe","cite":"125 S. Ct. 521","y":2004,"id":"2004/03-1669","sc":1,"sum":"In a unanimous per curiam opinion, the Court held that firing Roe for his behavior and \"speech\" did not violate the First Amendment. Government employers, the Court wrote, could restrict their employees' speech in ways that would be unconstitutional if applied to the general public. But government employees had the right to speak on matters of public concern, such as on government policies of interest to the public.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Sherrill v. Oneida Indian Nation","cite":"125 S. Ct. 1478","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-855","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that standards of federal Indian law and federal equity precluded the Tribe from unilaterally reviving its ancient sovereignty over the land at issue.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Clark v. Department of the Army","cite":"997 F.2d 1466","y":1993,"court":"Fed. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/997/1466/","by":{"0":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Connick v. Myers","cite":"461 U.S. 138","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-1251","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that speech of public employees is generally only protected when they speak on matters of public concern. Meyers’ speech only dealt with personal and internal office issues. The district court also erred in placing too high of a burden on Connick to show that Meyers’ speech substantially interfered with the operation of the office.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cook County Nat’l Bank v. United States","cite":"107 U.S. 445","y":1883,"id":"scdb/1882-191","sc":1,"sum":"This is an appeal from a decree of the Circuit Court overruling a general demurrer to a bill filed by the United States against the Cook County National Bank of Chicago, 111., and Augustus H. Burley, its receiver. The facts, as stated in the bill, are briefly as follows: Previously to 1872, the bank was formed under the acts of Congress authorizing the organization of national banks, and was designated as a …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cornelius v. NAACP Legal Def. & Educ. Fund, Inc.","cite":"473 U.S. 788","y":1985,"id":"1984/84-312","sc":1,"sum":"By Executive Order, participation in the Combined Federal Campaign (CFC), a charity drive aimed at federal employees, is limited to voluntary, tax-exempt, nonprofit charitable agencies that provide direct health und welfare services to individuals or their families, and legal defense and political advocacy organizations are specifically excluded. Participating organizations confine their fundraising activities to a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Demore v. Kim","cite":"538 U.S. 510","y":2003,"id":"2002/01-1491","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held, 6-3, that the Immigration and Nationality Act does not deprive the federal courts of jurisdiction to grant habeas relief to aliens challenging their detention under section 1226(c) and, 5-4, that detention during removal proceedings is a constitutionally permissible part of that process.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"47, 48"}},{"t":"case","label":"Director of Revenue v. CoBank ACB","cite":"531 U.S. 316","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1792","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that banks for cooperatives are subject to taxation. Justice Thomas wrote for the Court that nothing in the 1985 amendments to the Farm Credit Act indicated a repeal of the previous express approval of state taxation and that the structure of the Act indicated by negative implication that banks for cooperatives were not entitled to immunity.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dotson v. Griesa","cite":"398 F.3d 156","y":2005,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/398/156/","op":1,"by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dugan v. Ramsay","cite":"727 F.2d 192","y":1984,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/727/192/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"EEOC v. FLRA","cite":"476 U.S. 19","y":1986,"id":"1985/84-1728","sc":1,"sum":"The writ of certiorari previously granted by this Court is dismissed as improvidently granted. The EEOC did not assert before either the FLRA or the Court of Appeals its contentions here that (1) the Circular is not an \"applicable la[w]\" within the meaning of the Act's management rights clause, and therefore AFGE's proposal, by requiring compliance with the Circular, would intrude on management's reserved rights, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Eastern Bridge, LLC v. Chao","cite":"320 F.3d 84","y":2003,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/320/84/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Faragher v. City of Boca Raton","cite":"524 U.S. 775","y":1989,"id":"1997/97-282","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that an employer is vicariously liable under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 for actionable discrimination caused by a supervisor.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Fausto v. United States","cite":"791 F.2d 1554","y":1986,"court":"Fed. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/791/1554/","op":1,"ar":3,"by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Florida Power & Light Co. v. Lorion","cite":"470 U.S. 729","y":1985,"id":"1984/83-703","sc":1,"sum":"Section 2239 vests in the courts of appeals initial subject-matter jurisdiction over Commission orders denying citizen petitions made pursuant to Commission rules.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"50"}},{"t":"case","label":"Fornaro v. James","cite":"416 F.3d 63","y":2005,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/416/63/","by":{"0":"17, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gergick v. Austin","cite":"997 F.2d 1237","y":1993,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/997/1237/","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Graham v. Ashcroft","cite":"358 F.3d 391","y":2004,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/358/391/","by":{"0":"20, 22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Greenway v. United States","cite":"163 Ct. Cl. 72","y":1963,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ct.%20Cl./163/72/","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Griffith v. FLRA","cite":"842 F.2d 487","y":1988,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/842/487/","by":{"0":"49"}},{"t":"case","label":"HUD v. Rucker","cite":"535 U.S. 125","y":2002,"id":"2001/00-1770","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Anti-Drug Abuse Act of 1988 unambiguously requires lease terms that vest local public housing authorities with the discretion to evict tenants for the drug-related activity of household members and guests whether or not the tenant knew, or should have known, about the activity.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hall v. Clinton","cite":"235 F.3d 202","y":2000,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/235/202/","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hallock v. Moses","cite":"731 F.2d 754","y":1984,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/731/754/","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hardison v. Cohen","cite":"375 F.3d 1262","y":2004,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/375/1262/","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Harrison v. Bowen","cite":"815 F.2d 1505","y":1987,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/815/1505/","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Harrison v. PPG Indus., Inc.","cite":"446 U.S. 578","y":1980,"id":"1979/78-1918","sc":1,"sum":"The phrase \"any other final action\" in § 307 (b) (1) is to be construed in accordance with its literal meaning so as to reach any action of the Administrator that is final, not just final actions of the Administrator similar to actions under the specifically enumerated provisions that precede the catchall phrase in the statute.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hubbard v. EPA","cite":"809 F.2d 1","y":1987,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/809/1/","by":{"0":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ingram v. Secretary of HHS","cite":"830 F.2d 67","y":1987,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/830/67/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Johnson v. Peterson","cite":"996 F.2d 397","y":1993,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/996/397/","by":{"0":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jones v. TVA","cite":"948 F.2d 258","y":1991,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/948/258/","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Karahalios v. National Fed’n of Fed. Employees, Local 1263","cite":"489 U.S. 527","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-636","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Title VII of the CSRA does not confer on federal employees a private cause of action against a breach by a union representing such employees of its statutory duty of fair representation.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"12, 16, 19, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ledford v. West","cite":"136 F.3d 776","y":1998,"court":"Fed. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/136/776/","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Leefer v. Administrator, NASA","cite":"543 F.2d 209","y":1976,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/543/209/","sum":"Noting that employees were not eligible even for administrative review for suspensions of 30 days or less.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lehman v. Morrissey","cite":"779 F.2d 526","y":1985,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/779/526/","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lindahl v. OPM","cite":"470 U.S. 768","y":1985,"id":"1984/83-5954","sc":1,"sum":"The Office of Personnel Management (OPM) \"determine[s] questions of disability and dependency\" in administering the Federal Government's disability retirement program. 5 U. S. C. § 8347(c). Its \"decisions... concerning these matters are final and conclusive and are not subject to review,\" ibid., except to the extent that administrative review by the Merit Systems Protection Board (MSPB) is provided by §8347(d)(1). …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"2, 4, 19, 33, 48, 49"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lombardi v. SBA","cite":"889 F.2d 959","y":1989,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/889/959/","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lorillard v. Pons","cite":"434 U.S. 575","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-1346","sc":1,"by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mathews v. Eldridge","cite":"424 U.S. 319","y":1976,"id":"1975/74-204","sc":1,"sum":"In an 6-to-2 decision, the Court held that the initial termination of Eldridge's benefits without a hearing did not violate due process.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"McCarthy v. Madigan","cite":"503 U.S. 140","y":1992,"id":"1991/90-6861","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Exhaustion of the Bureau of Prisons' administrative procedure is not required before a federal prisoner can initiate a Bivens action solely for money damages.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"McDonald v. City of W. Branch","cite":"466 U.S. 284","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-219","sc":1,"sum":"In a § 1983 action, a federal court should not afford res judicata or collateral estoppel effect to an award in an arbitration proceeding brought pursuant to the terms of a collective-bargaining agreement, and hence petitioner's § 1983 action was not barred by the arbitration award.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"McKart v. United States","cite":"395 U.S. 185","y":1969,"id":"1968/403","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner had been classified IV-A (sole surviving son status). When the Selective Service Board learned of the death of petitioner's mother, it reclassified him I-A (available for military service), on the theory that the IV-A classification became improper when the \"family unit\" ceased to exist. Petitioner did not appeal the reclassification. Upon his failure to report as ordered for his pre-induction physical …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"39, 40"}},{"t":"case","label":"McNary v. Haitian Refugee Ctr., Inc.","cite":"498 U.S. 479","y":1991,"id":"1990/89-1332","sc":1,"sum":"The District Court had federal-question jurisdiction to hear respondents' constitutional and statutory challenges to the INS procedures.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Meredith Corp. v. FCC","cite":"809 F.2d 863","y":1987,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/809/863/","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mitchum v. Hurt","cite":"73 F.3d 30","y":1995,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/73/30/","sum":"Holding that CSRA did not foreclose suit seeking equitable relief for constitutional grievance.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Montplaisir v. Leighton","cite":"875 F.2d 1","y":1999,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/875/1/","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Morris v. Gressette","cite":"432 U.S. 491","y":1977,"id":"1976/75-1583","sc":1,"sum":"Section 5 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 establishes two alternative methods by which States subject to the Act can obtain federal preclearance review of a change in their voting laws: (1) the State may file a declaratory judgment action in the District Court for the District of Columbia and subsequently may implement such change if that court declares that the change has no racially discriminatory purpose or …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mudge v. United States","cite":"308 F.3d 1220","y":2002,"court":"Fed. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/308/1220/","by":{"0":"11, 17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Muniz v. United States","cite":"972 F.2d 1304","y":1992,"court":"Fed. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/972/1304/","sum":"Noting Congress’s “unambiguous and unmistakable preference for exclusivity of arbitration”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"19, 39"}},{"t":"case","label":"National Treasury Employees Union v. FLRA","cite":"986 F.2d 537","y":1993,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/986/537/","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"National Treasury Employees Union v. FLRA","cite":"112 F.3d 402","y":1997,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/112/402/","by":{"0":"49"}},{"t":"case","label":"National Treasury Employees Union v. Von Raab","cite":"489 U.S. 656","y":1989,"id":"1988/86-1879","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the \"substantial interests\" of the government in stifling the drug trade justified \"departure from the ordinary warrant and probable cause requirements\" associated with searches.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"37, 42"}},{"t":"case","label":"O'Connell v. Hove","cite":"22 F.3d 463","y":1994,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/22/463/","by":{"0":"23, 24, 25, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ohio v. Robinette","cite":"519 U.S. 33","y":1996,"id":"1996/95-891","sc":1,"sum":"After establishing its federal jurisdiction, despite a claim that the matter involved aspects of Ohio's Constitution, the Court held that when looking at the totality of the circumstances it may be reasonably concluded that if a defendant consents to be searched, even if not first advised that he is \"free to go,\" the ensuing search will be recognized as voluntary.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Parker v. King","cite":"935 F.2d 1174","y":1991,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/935/1174/","by":{"0":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Petrini v. Howard","cite":"918 F.2d 1482","y":1990,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/918/1482/","by":{"0":"17, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pinar v. Dole","cite":"747 F.2d 899","y":1984,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/747/899/","sum":"Holding that CSRA precludes equitable relief, at least where constitutional injury is not major.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"20, 21, 26, 32, 45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Premachandra v. United States","cite":"739 F.2d 392","y":1984,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/739/392/","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Public Citizen v. United States Dep’t of Justice","cite":"491 U.S. 440","y":1989,"id":"1988/88-429","sc":1,"sum":"To aid the President in fulfilling his constitutional duty to appoint federal judges, the Department of Justice regularly seeks advice from the Standing Committee on Federal Judiciary of the American Bar Association (ABA) regarding potential nominees for judgeships. The ABA Committee's investigations, reports, and votes on potential nominees are kept confidential, although its rating of a particular candidate is …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Public Util. Comm. v. United States","cite":"355 U.S. 534","y":1958,"id":"1957/23","sc":1,"sum":"California enacted, and its Public Utilities Commission plainly indicated an intent to enforce, a statute which would have made contingent upon the Commission's prior approval continuation of the Federal Government's long-established\" practice, sanctioned by federal law and regulations, of negotiating with common carriers special rates for the shipment of government property within the State..- The United States …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Quackenbush v. Allstate Ins. Co.","cite":"517 U.S. 706","y":1996,"id":"1995/95-244","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that an abstention-based remand order is appealable because the \"remand order here falls within that narrow class of collateral orders that are immediately appealable.\" Further, the Court held that the \"federal courts have the power to dismiss or remand cases based on abstention principles only where the relief sought is equitable or otherwise discretionary,\" and because \"this was a damages action, the District Court's remand order was an unwarranted application of the Burford doctrine.\" Justices Antonin Scalia and Anthony M. Kennedy wrote concurring opinions.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ryon v. O’Neill","cite":"894 F.2d 199","y":1990,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/894/199/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Salinas v. United States","cite":"323 F.3d 1047","y":2003,"court":"Fed. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/323/1047/","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sampson v. Murray","cite":"415 U.S. 61","y":1974,"id":"1973/72-403","sc":1,"sum":"Upon being notified that she was' going to be discharged on a specific date from her position as aprobationary Government employee, respondent filed this action claiming that the applicable Civil Service regulations for discharge of probationary employees had not been followed, and seeking a temporary injunction against her dismissal pending an administrative appeal to the Civil Service Commission (CSC). The …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Saul v. United States","cite":"928 F.2d 829","y":1991,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/928/829/","by":{"0":"17, 25, 32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Schrachta v. Curtis","cite":"752 F.2d 1257","y":1985,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/752/1257/","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Smith v. Phillips","cite":"455 U.S. 209","y":1982,"id":"1981/80-1082","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent was not denied due process of law either by the juror's conduct or by the prosecutors' failure to disclose the juror's job application.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Spagnola v. Mathis","cite":"859 F.2d 223","y":1988,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/859/223/","by":{"0":"17, 21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Steadman v. Governor, United States Soldiers’ & Airmen’s Home","cite":"918 F.2d 963","y":1990,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/918/963/","by":{"0":"23, 32, 40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stephens v. HHS","cite":"901 F.2d 1571","y":1990,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/901/1571/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sturges v. Crowninshield","cite":"17 U.S. 122","y":1819,"id":"scdb/1819-012","sc":1,"sum":"Sturges v. Crowninshield.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Suzal v. Director, USIA","cite":"32 F.3d 574","y":1994,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/32/574/","by":{"0":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Switchmen’s Union v. National Mediation Bd.","cite":"320 U.S. 297","y":1943,"id":"scdb/1943-013","sc":1,"sum":"that the District Court was without jurisdiction to review the action of the Board in issuing the certificate.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"15, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Taydus v. Cisneros","cite":"902 F. Supp. 288","y":1995,"court":"D. Mass.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp./902/288/","sum":"Stating that, in light of Fausto and more recent circuit precedent, it is likely that “the First Circuit would not find a cause of action under the APA if faced with the issue today”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Thunder Basin Coal Co. v. Reich","cite":"510 U.S. 200","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-896","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner mine operator's nonunion work force designated two employees of the United Mine Workers of America (UMWA) to serve as miners' representatives under 30 U. S. C. §813(f). Claiming that the designations compromised its rights under the National Labor Relations Act (NLRA), petitioner refused to post information about the representatives as required by a regulation issued by the Department of Labor's Mine …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"35, 36, 38, 39, 42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Tiltti v. Weise","cite":"155 F.3d 596","y":1998,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/155/596/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Booker","cite":"125 S. Ct. 738","y":2005,"id":"2004/04-104","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Sentencing Guidelines, where they allow judges to enhance sentences using facts not reviewed by juries, violated the Sixth Amendment right to trial by jury. The sentences of Booker and Fanfan, based partly on facts determined only by judges, were therefore unconstitutional.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Craft","cite":"535 U.S. 278","y":2002,"id":"2001/00-1831","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that each tenant possesses individual rights in the estate sufficient to constitute \"property\" or \"rights to property\" for the purposes of the lien.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Erika, Inc.","cite":"456 U.S. 201","y":1982,"id":"1981/80-1594","sc":1,"sum":"The Court of Claims has no jurisdiction to review determinations by private insurance carriers of the amount of benefits payable under Part B of the Medicare program.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"passim"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Testan","cite":"424 U.S. 392","y":1976,"id":"1975/74-753","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent Government trial attorneys with civil service grade GS-13 classifications requested their employing agency to reclassify their positions to grade GS-14, contending that their duties and responsibilities met the requirements for the higher grade and were identical to those of other trial attorneys classified as GS-14 in another agency, and that under the principle of \"equal pay for substantially equal …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States Dep’t of the Treasury v. FLRA","cite":"43 F.3d 682","y":1995,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/43/682/","by":{"0":"48"}},{"t":"case","label":"Veit v. Heckler","cite":"746 F.2d 508","y":1984,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/746/508/","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Weatherford v. Dole","cite":"763 F.2d 392","y":1985,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/763/392/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Webster v. Doe","cite":"486 U.S. 592","y":1998,"id":"1987/86-1294","sc":1,"ar":2,"sum":"Section 102(c) of the National Security Act of 1947 (NSA) authorizes the Director of the Central Intelligence Agency (CIA), \"in his discretion,\" to terminate the employment of any CIA employee \"whenever he shall deem such termination necessary or advisable in the interests of the United States.\" After respondent, a covert electronics technician in the CIA's employ, voluntarily informed the agency that he was a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"18, 47"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment IV","clauses":[["fourth-amendment","Fourth Amendment"]],"text":"The right of the people to be secure in their persons, houses, papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches and seizures, shall not be violated, and no Warrants shall issue, but upon probable cause, supported by Oath or affirmation, and particularly describing the place to be searched, and the persons or things to be seized.","by":{"0":"35, 42"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 103-424","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/108/4361","by":{"0":"29"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 701","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/701","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"0":"12, 15, 18, 20, 36, 12, 18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 702","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/702","by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 5596","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/5596","by":{"0":"12, 15, 16, 17, 18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"42 U.S.C. § 2000e","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/2000e","by":{"0":"6, 8, 18, 19, 23"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 1101","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/1101","by":{"0":"2"}},{"t":"usc","label":"29 U.S.C. § 201","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/201","by":{"0":"24, 25"}},{"t":"usc","label":"29 U.S.C. § 216","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/216","subs":["(b)"],"by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 106","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/106","by":{"0":"3, 3"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 40122","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/40122","subs":["(g)","(h)","(g)(1)","(g)(2)","(g)(2)(A)","(g)(2)(C)","(g)(2)(H)","(g)(3)","(i)","(j)"],"stext":{"head":"§40122. Federal Aviation Administration personnel management system","paras":[[0,"(a) In General.—"],[1,"(1) Consultation and negotiation.—In developing and making changes to the personnel management system initially implemented by the Administrator of the Federal Aviation Administration on April 1, 1996, the Administrator shall negotiate with the exclusive bargaining representatives of employees of the Administration certified under section 7111 of title 5 and consult with other employees of the Administration."],[1,"(2) Dispute resolution.—"],[2,"(A) Mediation.—If the Administrator does not reach an agreement under paragraph (1) or the provisions referred to in subsection (g)(2)(C) with the exclusive bargaining representative of the employees, the Administrator and the bargaining representative—"],[3,"(i) shall use the services of the Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service to attempt to reach such agreement in accordance with part 1425 of title 29, Code of Federal Regulations (as in effect on the date of enactment of the FAA Modernization and Reform Act of 2012); or"],[3,"(ii) may by mutual agreement adopt alternative procedures for the resolution of disputes or impasses arising in the negotiation of the collective-bargaining agreement."],[2,"(B) Mid-term bargaining.—If the services of the Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service under subparagraph (A)(i) do not lead to the resolution of issues in controversy arising from the negotiation of a mid-term collective-bargaining agreement, the Federal Service Impasses Panel shall assist the parties in resolving the impasse in accordance with section 7119 of title 5."],[2,"(C) Binding arbitration for term bargaining.—"],[3,"(i) Assistance from federal service impasses panel.—If the services of the Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service under subparagraph (A)(i) do not lead to the resolution of issues in controversy arising from the negotiation of a term collective-bargaining agreement, the Administrator and the exclusive bargaining representative of the employees (in this subparagraph referred to as the \"parties\") shall submit their issues in controversy to the Federal Service Impasses Panel. The Panel shall assist the parties in resolving the impasse by asserting jurisdiction and ordering binding arbitration by a private arbitration board consisting of 3 members."],[3,"(ii) Appointment of arbitration board.—The Executive Director of the Panel shall provide for the appointment of the 3 members of a private arbitration board under clause (i) by requesting the Director of the Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service to prepare a list of not less than 15 names of arbitrators with Federal sector experience and by providing the list to the parties. Not later than 10 days after receiving the list, the parties shall each select one person from the list. The 2 arbitrators selected by the parties shall then select a third person from the list not later than 7 days after being selected. If either of the parties fails to select a person or if the 2 arbitrators are unable to agree on the third person in 7 days, the parties shall make the selection by alternately striking names on the list until one arbitrator remains."],[3,"(iii) Framing issues in controversy.—If the parties do not agree on the framing of the issues to be submitted for arbitration, the arbitration board shall frame the issues."],[3,"(iv) Hearings.—The arbitration board shall give the parties a full and fair hearing, including an opportunity to present evidence in support of their claims and an opportunity to present their case in person, by counsel, or by other representative as they may elect."],[3,"(v) Decisions.—The arbitration board shall render its decision within 90 days after the date of its appointment. Decisions of the arbitration board shall be conclusive and binding upon the parties."],[3,"(vi) Matters for consideration.—The arbitration board shall take into consideration such factors as—"],[4,"(I) the effect of its arbitration decisions on the Federal Aviation Administration's ability to attract and retain a qualified workforce;"],[4,"(II) the effect of its arbitration decisions on the Federal Aviation Administration's budget; and"],[4,"(III) any other factors whose consideration would assist the board in fashioning a fair and equitable award."],[3,"(vii) Costs.—The parties shall share costs of the arbitration equally."],[1,"(3) Ratification of agreements.—Upon reaching a voluntary agreement or at the conclusion of the binding arbitration under paragraph (2)(C), the final agreement, except for those matters decided by an arbitration board, shall be subject to ratification by the exclusive bargaining representative of the employees, if so requested by the bargaining representative, and the final agreement shall be subject to approval by the head of the agency in accordance with the provisions referred to in subsection (g)(2)(C)."],[1,"(4) Cost savings and productivity goals.—The Administration and the exclusive bargaining representatives of the employees shall use every reasonable effort to find cost savings and to increase productivity within each of the affected bargaining units."],[1,"(5) Annual budget discussions.—The Administration and the exclusive bargaining representatives of the employees shall meet annually for the purpose of finding additional cost savings within the Administration's annual budget as it applies to each of the affected bargaining units and throughout the agency."],[0,"(b) Expert Evaluation.—On the date that is 3 years after the personnel management system is implemented, the Administration shall employ outside experts to provide an independent evaluation of the effectiveness of the system within 3 months after such date. For this purpose, the Administrator may utilize the services of experts and consultants under section 3109 of title 5 without regard to the limitation imposed by the last sentence of section 3109(b) of such title, and may contract on a sole source basis, notwithstanding any other provision of law to the contrary."],[0,"(c) Pay Restriction.—No officer or employee of the Administration may receive an annual rate of basic pay in excess of the annual rate of basic pay payable to the Administrator."],[0,"(d) Ethics.—The Administration shall be subject to Executive Order No. 12674 and regulations and opinions promulgated by the Office of Government Ethics, including those set forth in section 2635 of title 5 of the Code of Federal Regulations."],[0,"(e) Employee Protections.—Until July 1, 1999, basic wages (including locality pay) and operational differential pay provided employees of the Administration shall not be involuntarily adversely affected by reason of the enactment of this section, except for unacceptable performance or by reason of a reduction in force or reorganization or by agreement between the Administration and the affected employees' exclusive bargaining representative."],[0,"(f) Labor-Management Agreements.—Except as otherwise provided by this title, all labor-management agreements covering employees of the Administration that are in effect on the effective date of the Air Traffic Management System Performance Improvement Act of 1996 shall remain in effect until their normal expiration date, unless the Administrator and the exclusive bargaining representative agree to the contrary."],[0,"(g) Personnel Management System.—"],[1,"(1) In general.—In consultation with the employees of the Administration and such non-governmental experts in personnel management systems as he may employ, and notwithstanding the provisions of title 5 and other Federal personnel laws, the Administrator shall develop and implement, not later than January 1, 1996, a personnel management system for the Administration that addresses the unique demands on the agency's workforce. Such a new system shall, at a minimum, provide for greater flexibility in the hiring, training, compensation, and location of personnel."],[1,"(2) Applicability of title 5.—The provisions of title 5 shall not apply to the new personnel management system developed and implemented pursuant to paragraph (1), with the exception of—"],[2,"(A) section 2302(b), relating to whistleblower protection, including the provisions for investigation and enforcement as provided in chapter 12 of title 5;"],[2,"(B) sections 3304(f),1 to the extent consistent with the Federal Aviation Administration's status as an excepted service agency, 3308–3320, 3330a, 3330b, 3330c, and 3330d, relating to veterans' preference;"],[2,"(C) chapter 71, relating to labor-management relations;"],[2,"(D) section 7204, relating to antidiscrimination;"],[2,"(E) chapter 73, relating to suitability, security, and conduct;"],[2,"(F) chapter 81, relating to compensation for work injury;"],[2,"(G) chapters 83–85, 87, and 89, relating to retirement, unemployment compensation, and insurance coverage;"],[2,"(H) sections 1204, 1211–1218, 1221, and 7701–7703, relating to the Merit Systems Protection Board;"],[2,"(I) subsections (b), (c), and (d) of section 4507 (relating to Meritorious Executive or Distinguished Executive rank awards) and subsections (b) and (c) of section 4507a (relating to Meritorious Senior Professional or Distinguished Senior Professional rank awards), except that—"],[3,"(i) for purposes of applying such provisions to the personnel management system—"]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/49/40122?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":2,"head":"§40122. Federal Aviation Administration personnel management system","tname":"Transportation","by":{"0":"2, 2, 2, 3, 14, 3, 4, 5, 6, 6, 4, 5, 35, 4, 21, 4, 4, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"21 U.S.C. § 301","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/21/301","by":{"0":"36"}},{"t":"usc","label":"30 U.S.C. § 801","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/30/801","by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1491","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1491","by":{"0":"12, 16, 17, 18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 1201","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/1201","by":{"0":"5"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 1204","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/1204","by":{"0":"5"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 1211","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/1211","by":{"0":"5"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 1214","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/1214","subs":["(a)(1)","(a)(1)(D)","(a)(2)","(a)(3)","(b)(1)(A)(i)","(b)(2)","(c)","(c)(1)"],"by":{"0":"5, 6, 6, 5, 36, 5, 35, 6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 1221","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/1221","by":{"0":"5, 6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 2301","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/2301","subs":["(b)(2)"],"by":{"0":"4, 5, 34, 5"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 2302","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/2302","subs":["(a)(2)(A)","(b)","(b)(1)","(b)(2)","(b)(7)","(b)(8)","(b)(12)","(d)"],"stext":{"head":"§2302. Prohibited personnel practices","paras":[[0,"(a)(1) For the purpose of this title, \"prohibited personnel practice\" means any action described in subsection (b)."],[0,"(2) For the purpose of this section—"],[1,"(A) \"personnel action\" means—"],[2,"(i) an appointment;"],[2,"(ii) a promotion;"],[2,"(iii) an action under chapter 75 of this title or other disciplinary or corrective action;"],[2,"(iv) a detail, transfer, or reassignment;"],[2,"(v) a reinstatement;"],[2,"(vi) a restoration;"],[2,"(vii) a reemployment;"],[2,"(viii) a performance evaluation under chapter 43 of this title or under title 38;"],[2,"(ix) a decision concerning pay, benefits, or awards, or concerning education or training if the education or training may reasonably be expected to lead to an appointment, promotion, performance evaluation, or other action described in this subparagraph;"],[2,"(x) a decision to order psychiatric testing or examination;"],[2,"(xi) the implementation or enforcement of any nondisclosure policy, form, or agreement; and"],[2,"(xii) any other significant change in duties, responsibilities, or working conditions;"],[1,"with respect to an employee in, or applicant for, a covered position in an agency, and in the case of an alleged prohibited personnel practice described in subsection (b)(8), an employee or applicant for employment in a Government corporation as defined in section 9101 of title 31;"],[1,"(B) \"covered position\" means, with respect to any personnel action, any position in the competitive service, a career appointee position in the Senior Executive Service, or a position in the excepted service, but does not include any position which is, prior to the personnel action—"],[2,"(i) excepted from the competitive service because of its confidential, policy-determining, policy-making, or policy-advocating character; or"],[2,"(ii) excluded from the coverage of this section by the President based on a determination by the President that it is necessary and warranted by conditions of good administration;"],[1,"(C) \"agency\" means an Executive agency and the Government Publishing Office, but does not include—"],[2,"(i) a Government corporation, except in the case of an alleged prohibited personnel practice described under subsection (b)(8) or section 2302(b)(9)(A)(i), (B), (C), or (D);"],[2,"(ii)(I) the Federal Bureau of Investigation, the Central Intelligence Agency, the Defense Intelligence Agency, the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency, the National Security Agency, the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, and the National Reconnaissance Office; and"],[2,"(II) as determined by the President, any executive agency or unit thereof the principal function of which is the conduct of foreign intelligence or counterintelligence activities, provided that the determination be made prior to a personnel action; or"],[2,"(iii) the Government Accountability Office; and"],[1,"(D) \"disclosure\" means a formal or informal communication or transmission, but does not include a communication concerning policy decisions that lawfully exercise discretionary authority unless the employee or applicant providing the disclosure reasonably believes that the disclosure evidences—"],[2,"(i) any violation of any law, rule, or regulation; or"],[2,"(ii) gross mismanagement, a gross waste of funds, an abuse of authority, or a substantial and specific danger to public health or safety."],[0,"(b) Any employee who has authority to take, direct others to take, recommend, or approve any personnel action, shall not, with respect to such authority—"],[1,"(1) discriminate for or against any employee or applicant for employment—"],[2,"(A) on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, or national origin, as prohibited under section 717 of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 (42 U.S.C. 2000e–16);"],[2,"(B) on the basis of age, as prohibited under sections 12 and 15 of the Age Discrimination in Employment Act of 1967 (29 U.S.C. 631, 633a);"],[2,"(C) on the basis of sex, as prohibited under section 6(d) of the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 (29 U.S.C. 206(d));"],[2,"(D) on the basis of handicapping condition, as prohibited under section 501 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 (29 U.S.C. 791); or"],[2,"(E) on the basis of marital status or political affiliation, as prohibited under any law, rule, or regulation;"],[1,"(2) solicit or consider any recommendation or statement, oral or written, with respect to any individual who requests or is under consideration for any personnel action unless such recommendation or statement is based on the personal knowledge or records of the person furnishing it and consists of—"],[2,"(A) an evaluation of the work performance, ability, aptitude, or general qualifications of such individual; or"],[2,"(B) an evaluation of the character, loyalty, or suitability of such individual;"],[1,"(3) coerce the political activity of any person (including the providing of any political contribution or service), or take any action against any employee or applicant for employment as a reprisal for the refusal of any person to engage in such political activity;"],[1,"(4) deceive or willfully obstruct any person with respect to such person's right to compete for employment;"],[1,"(5) influence any person to withdraw from competition for any position for the purpose of improving or injuring the prospects of any other person for employment;"],[1,"(6) grant any preference or advantage not authorized by law, rule, or regulation to any employee or applicant for employment (including defining the scope or manner of competition or the requirements for any position) for the purpose of improving or injuring the prospects of any particular person for employment;"],[1,"(7) appoint, employ, promote, advance, or advocate for appointment, employment, promotion, or advancement, in or to a civilian position any individual who is a relative (as defined in section 3110(a)(3) of this title) of such employee if such position is in the agency in which such employee is serving as a public official (as defined in section 3110(a)(2) of this title) or over which such employee exercises jurisdiction or control as such an official;"],[1,"(8) take or fail to take, or threaten to take or fail to take, a personnel action with respect to any employee or applicant for employment because of—"],[2,"(A) any disclosure of information by an employee or applicant which the employee or applicant reasonably believes evidences—"],[3,"(i) any violation of any law, rule, or regulation, or"],[3,"(ii) gross mismanagement, a gross waste of funds, an abuse of authority, or a substantial and specific danger to public health or safety,"],[2,"if such disclosure is not specifically prohibited by law and if such information is not specifically required by Executive order to be kept secret in the interest of national defense or the conduct of foreign affairs;"],[2,"(B) any disclosure to the Special Counsel, or to the Inspector General of an agency or another employee designated by the head of the agency to receive such disclosures, of information which the employee or applicant reasonably believes evidences—"],[3,"(i) any violation (other than a violation of this section) of any law, rule, or regulation, or"],[3,"(ii) gross mismanagement, a gross waste of funds, an abuse of authority, or a substantial and specific danger to public health or safety; or"],[2,"(C) any disclosure to Congress (including any committee of Congress) by any employee of an agency or applicant for employment at an agency of information described in subparagraph (B) that is—"],[3,"(i) not classified; or"],[3,"(ii) if classified—"],[4,"(I) has been classified by the head of an agency that is not an element of the intelligence community (as defined by section 3 of the National Security Act of 1947 (50 U.S.C. 3003)); and"],[4,"(II) does not reveal intelligence sources and methods.1"],[1,"(9) take or fail to take, or threaten to take or fail to take, any personnel action against any employee or applicant for employment because of—"],[2,"(A) the exercise of any appeal, complaint, or grievance right granted by any law, rule, or regulation—"],[3,"(i) with regard to remedying a violation of paragraph (8); or"],[3,"(ii) other than with regard to remedying a violation of paragraph (8);"],[2,"(B) testifying for or otherwise lawfully assisting any individual in the exercise of any right referred to in subparagraph (A)(i) or (ii);"],[2,"(C) cooperating with or disclosing information to the Inspector General (or any other component responsible for internal investigation or review) of an agency, or the Special Counsel, in accordance with applicable provisions of law; or"],[2,"(D) refusing to obey an order that would require the individual to violate a law, rule, or regulation;"],[1,"(10) discriminate for or against any employee or applicant for employment on the basis of conduct which does not adversely affect the performance of the employee or applicant or the performance of others; except that nothing in this paragraph shall prohibit an agency from taking into account in determining suitability or fitness any conviction of the employee or applicant for any crime under the laws of any State, of the District of Columbia, or of the United States;"],[1,"(11)(A) knowingly take, recommend, or approve any personnel action if the taking of such action would violate a veterans' preference requirement; or"],[1,"(B) knowingly fail to take, recommend, or approve any personnel action if the failure to take such action would violate a veterans' preference requirement;"]],"more":true,"ed":"2025","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/5/2302?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§2302. Prohibited personnel practices","tname":"Government Organization and Employees","by":{"0":"5, 5, 5, 5, 8, 5, 34, 5, 5, 6, 5, 34, 6, 19, 23"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 4301","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/4301","by":{"0":"4, 8"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 4303","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/4303","subs":["(b)","(e)"],"by":{"0":"7, 18, 28, 4, 4, 21"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7101","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7101","by":{"0":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7102","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7102","by":{"0":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7103","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7103","subs":["(a)(1)","(a)(9)(A)","(a)(9)(C)","(a)(9)(C)(ii)"],"by":{"0":"7, 7, 43, 24, 7"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7111","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7111","by":{"0":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7114","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7114","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"0":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7117","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7117","by":{"0":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7118","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7118","by":{"0":"23, 48"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7121","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7121","subs":["(a)","(a)(1)","(a)(1)(1988)","(a)(2)","(b)","(b)(1)(C)(iii)","(b)(2)(B)","(c)","(d)","(e)","(f)","(g)"],"stext":{"head":"§7121. Grievance procedures","paras":[[0,"(a)(1) Except as provided in paragraph (2) of this subsection, any collective bargaining agreement shall provide procedures for the settlement of grievances, including questions of arbitrability. Except as provided in subsections (d), (e), and (g) of this section, the procedures shall be the exclusive administrative procedures for resolving grievances which fall within its coverage."],[0,"(2) Any collective bargaining agreement may exclude any matter from the application of the grievance procedures which are provided for in the agreement."],[0,"(b)(1) Any negotiated grievance procedure referred to in subsection (a) of this section shall—"],[1,"(A) be fair and simple,"],[1,"(B) provide for expeditious processing, and"],[1,"(C) include procedures that—"],[2,"(i) assure an exclusive representative the right, in its own behalf or on behalf of any employee in the unit represented by the exclusive representative, to present and process grievances;"],[2,"(ii) assure such an employee the right to present a grievance on the employee's own behalf, and assure the exclusive representative the right to be present during the grievance proceeding; and"],[2,"(iii) provide that any grievance not satisfactorily settled under the negotiated grievance procedure shall be subject to binding arbitration which may be invoked by either the exclusive representative or the agency."],[0,"(2)(A) The provisions of a negotiated grievance procedure providing for binding arbitration in accordance with paragraph (1)(C)(iii) shall, if or to the extent that an alleged prohibited personnel practice is involved, allow the arbitrator to order—"],[1,"(i) a stay of any personnel action in a manner similar to the manner described in section 1221(c) with respect to the Merit Systems Protection Board; and"],[1,"(ii) the taking, by an agency, of any disciplinary action identified under section 1215(a)(3) that is otherwise within the authority of such agency to take."],[0,"(B) Any employee who is the subject of any disciplinary action ordered under subparagraph (A)(ii) may appeal such action to the same extent and in the same manner as if the agency had taken the disciplinary action absent arbitration."],[0,"(c) The preceding subsections of this section shall not apply with respect to any grievance concerning—"],[1,"(1) any claimed violation of subchapter III of chapter 73 of this title (relating to prohibited political activities);"],[1,"(2) retirement, life insurance, or health insurance;"],[1,"(3) a suspension or removal under section 7532 of this title;"],[1,"(4) any examination, certification, or appointment; or"],[1,"(5) the classification of any position which does not result in the reduction in grade or pay of an employee."],[0,"(d) An aggrieved employee affected by a prohibited personnel practice under section 2302(b)(1) of this title which also falls under the coverage of the negotiated grievance procedure may raise the matter under a statutory procedure or the negotiated procedure, but not both. An employee shall be deemed to have exercised his option under this subsection to raise the matter under either a statutory procedure or the negotiated procedure at such time as the employee timely initiates an action under the applicable statutory procedure or timely files a grievance in writing, in accordance with the provisions of the parties' negotiated procedure, whichever event occurs first. Selection of the negotiated procedure in no manner prejudices the right of an aggrieved employee to request the Merit Systems Protection Board to review the final decision pursuant to section 7702 of this title in the case of any personnel action that could have been appealed to the Board, or, where applicable, to request the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission to review a final decision in any other matter involving a complaint of discrimination of the type prohibited by any law administered by the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission."],[0,"(e)(1) Matters covered under sections 4303 and 7512 of this title which also fall within the coverage of the negotiated grievance procedure may, in the discretion of the aggrieved employee, be raised either under the appellate procedures of section 7701 of this title or under the negotiated grievance procedure, but not both. Similar matters which arise under other personnel systems applicable to employees covered by this chapter may, in the discretion of the aggrieved employee, be raised either under the appellate procedures, if any, applicable to those matters, or under the negotiated grievance procedure, but not both. An employee shall be deemed to have exercised his option under this subsection to raise a matter either under the applicable appellate procedures or under the negotiated grievance procedure at such time as the employee timely files a notice of appeal under the applicable appellate procedures or timely files a grievance in writing in accordance with the provisions of the parties' negotiated grievance procedure, whichever event occurs first."],[0,"(2) In matters covered under sections 4303 and 7512 of this title which have been raised under the negotiated grievance procedure in accordance with this section, an arbitrator shall be governed by section 7701(c)(1) of this title, as applicable."],[0,"(f) In matters covered under sections 4303 and 7512 of this title which have been raised under the negotiated grievance procedure in accordance with this section, section 7703 of this title pertaining to judicial review shall apply to the award of an arbitrator in the same manner and under the same conditions as if the matter had been decided by the Board. In matters similar to those covered under sections 4303 and 7512 of this title which arise under other personnel systems and which an aggrieved employee has raised under the negotiated grievance procedure, judicial review of an arbitrator's award may be obtained in the same manner and on the same basis as could be obtained of a final decision in such matters raised under applicable appellate procedures."],[0,"(g)(1) This subsection applies with respect to a prohibited personnel practice other than a prohibited personnel practice to which subsection (d) applies."],[0,"(2) An aggrieved employee affected by a prohibited personnel practice described in paragraph (1) may elect not more than one of the remedies described in paragraph (3) with respect thereto. For purposes of the preceding sentence, a determination as to whether a particular remedy has been elected shall be made as set forth under paragraph (4)."],[0,"(3) The remedies described in this paragraph are as follows:"],[1,"(A) An appeal to the Merit Systems Protection Board under section 7701."],[1,"(B) A negotiated grievance procedure under this section."],[1,"(C) Procedures for seeking corrective action under subchapters II and III of chapter 12."],[0,"(4) For the purpose of this subsection, a person shall be considered to have elected—"],[1,"(A) the remedy described in paragraph (3)(A) if such person has timely filed a notice of appeal under the applicable appellate procedures;"],[1,"(B) the remedy described in paragraph (3)(B) if such person has timely filed a grievance in writing, in accordance with the provisions of the parties' negotiated procedure; or"],[1,"(C) the remedy described in paragraph (3)(C) if such person has sought corrective action from the Office of Special Counsel by making an allegation under section 1214(a)(1)."],[0,"(h) Settlements and awards under this chapter shall be subject to the limitations in section 5596(b)(4) of this title."]],"more":false,"ed":"2025","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/5/7121?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":3,"head":"§7121. Grievance procedures","tname":"Government Organization and Employees","by":{"0":"8, 25, 8, 25, 8, 25, 8, 25, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7122","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7122","subs":["(a)","(a)(1)"],"by":{"0":"4, 4, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7123","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7123","subs":["(a)","(a)(1)","(b)"],"by":{"0":"4, 4, 4, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7501","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7501","by":{"0":"4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7502","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7502","by":{"0":"4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7503","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7503","subs":["(b)"],"by":{"0":"4, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7512","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7512","by":{"0":"4, 7, 8, 18, 28"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7513","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7513","subs":["(b)","(d)"],"by":{"0":"4, 4, 21"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7701","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7701","by":{"0":"5, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7702","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7702","by":{"0":"23"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 7703","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/7703","subs":["(a)(1)","(b)","(b)(1)","(b)(2)"],"by":{"0":"7, 8, 21, 21, 35, 4, 6, 21, 24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 5331","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/5331","subs":["(d)(8)"],"by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 45101","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/45101","by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 45102","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/45102","subs":["(b)","(b)(1)"],"by":{"0":"42, 9"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 45104","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/45104","subs":["(8)"],"stext":{"head":"§45104. Testing and laboratory requirements","paras":[[0,"In carrying out section 45102 of this title, the Administrator of the Federal Aviation Administration shall develop requirements that—"],[1,"(1) promote, to the maximum extent practicable, individual privacy in the collection of specimens;"],[1,"(2) for laboratories and testing procedures for controlled substances, incorporate the Department of Health and Human Services scientific and technical guidelines dated April 11, 1988, and any amendments to those guidelines, including mandatory guidelines establishing—"],[2,"(A) comprehensive standards for every aspect of laboratory controlled substances testing and laboratory procedures to be applied in carrying out this chapter, including standards requiring the use of the best available technology to ensure the complete reliability and accuracy of controlled substances tests and strict procedures governing the chain of custody of specimens collected for controlled substances testing;"],[2,"(B) the minimum list of controlled substances for which individuals may be tested; and"],[2,"(C) appropriate standards and procedures for periodic review of laboratories and criteria for certification and revocation of certification of laboratories to perform controlled substances testing in carrying out this chapter;"],[1,"(3) require that a laboratory involved in controlled substances testing under this chapter have the capability and facility, at the laboratory, of performing screening and confirmation tests;"],[1,"(4) provide that all tests indicating the use of alcohol or a controlled substance in violation of law or a United States Government regulation be confirmed by a scientifically recognized method of testing capable of providing quantitative information about alcohol or a controlled substance;"],[1,"(5) provide that each specimen be subdivided, secured, and labeled in the presence of the tested individual and that a part of the specimen be retained in a secure manner to prevent the possibility of tampering, so that if the individual's confirmation test results are positive the individual has an opportunity to have the retained part tested by a 2d confirmation test done independently at another certified laboratory if the individual requests the 2d confirmation test not later than 3 days after being advised of the results of the first confirmation test;"],[1,"(6) ensure appropriate safeguards for testing to detect and quantify alcohol in breath and body fluid samples, including urine and blood, through the development of regulations that may be necessary and in consultation with the Secretary of Health and Human Services;"],[1,"(7) provide for the confidentiality of test results and medical information (except information about alcohol or a controlled substance) of employees, except that this clause does not prevent the use of test results for the orderly imposition of appropriate sanctions under this chapter; and"],[1,"(8) ensure that employees are selected for tests by nondiscriminatory and impartial methods, so that no employee is harassed by being treated differently from other employees in similar circumstances."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/49/45104?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§45104. Testing and laboratory requirements","tname":"Transportation","by":{"0":"14, 35, 40, 42"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 46110","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/46110","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"0":"4"}},{"t":"reg","label":"Federal Aviation Admin., FAA Personnel Management System (Mar. 28, 1996) <http://www.faa.gov/ahr/policy/PMS/ personel.htm> . . . . . . . . ., 60 Fed. Reg. 47,039","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/fr/60/47039","by":{"0":"27"}}]};